"How many people live in your town?": "1860cffd3e73",
"Imagine giving every person on Earth a seat on a giant map. In 2025, the United Nations estimates, there are about 8.23 billion of us. If people were spread out evenly, every piece of land would have its share. They are not. Some places are packed shoulder to shoulder, and enormous areas have almost nobody at all.": "16b1ef00bba3",
"Imagine giving every person on Earth a seat on a giant map. In 2025, the United Nations estimates, there are about 8.23 billion of us. If people were spread out evenly, every piece of land would have its share. They are not. Some places are packed shoulder to shoulder, and enormous areas have almost nobody at all. On the globe in this lesson, each dot stands for one million people. The dots crowd together in South Asia and East Asia, along the great rivers, and near many coasts. More than half of the world's people live in Asia. In the population data used here, about 86 out of every 100 people live north of the equator, partly because most of the world's land is in the Northern Hemisphere. Why there? People need water to drink and to grow food. They need land that is flat enough to farm and to build on, and a climate that is neither too dry nor too cold. Rivers and coasts also make it easier to travel and to trade. Where water, flat land, and a gentle climate come together, villages grow into towns, and towns grow into cities. Now look at the empty places. Deserts are too dry. High mountains and plateaus are cold, steep, and short of air. In the polar zones, as Lesson 7 showed, the sunlight is always slanted, and the ground may be frozen. The large deserts outlined on the globe cover about 10% of the land, but only about 2% of the world's people live in them, and most of those live along rivers such as the Nile. Land more than 3,000 meters high covers about 3% of the land and holds about 0.5% of the people. Low land matters, too. A 1998 study found that about a third of the world's people lived less than 100 meters above sea level, even though only about a sixth of the land where people live is that low. The population and height data on this page give almost the same answer: about 34% of people on about 14% of the land. A 2016 study estimated that about 40 percent of people live within 100 kilometers of a coast. To compare places, geographers use population density: the number of people divided by the area of land. Taiwan is one of the crowded places. In 2024, Taiwan had about 646 people per square kilometer, and Taipei City had more than 9,000. But even in Taiwan, people are not spread evenly: most live on the plains and in the basins, and few live high in the mountains.": "7d6d15f7c963",
"Low land matters, too. A 1998 study found that about a third of the world's people lived less than 100 meters above sea level, even though only about a sixth of the land where people live is that low. The population and height data on this page give almost the same answer: about 34% of people on about 14% of the land. A 2016 study estimated that about 40 percent of people live within 100 kilometers of a coast.": "95beb2e14325",
